      <option>Texas</option>
    </select>
    <div id="validationTooltip04Feedback" class="invalid-tooltip">
      Please select a valid state.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="col-md-3 position-relative">
    <label for="validationTooltip05" class="form-label">Zip</label>
    <input type="text" class="form-control" id="validationTooltip05" autocomplete="postal-code" aria-describedby="validationTooltip05Feedback" required>
    <div id="validationTooltip05Feedback" class="invalid-tooltip">
      Please provide a valid zip.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="col-12">
    <button class="btn btn-primary" type="submit">Submit form</button>
  </div>
</form>`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Validation · Bootstrap HTML"
  description="Bootstrap form validation with :user-valid and :user-invalid — no was-validated wrapper, no JavaScript."
>
  <h1>Validation</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Bootstrap’s
    <a href="https://getbootstrap.com/docs/5.3/forms/validation/">
      form validation
    </a>
    look, driven by
    <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Selectors/:user-valid">
      <code>:user-valid</code>
    </a>
    and
    <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Selectors/:user-invalid">
      <code>:user-invalid</code>
    </a>
    after the user types, blurs, or tries to submit. No
    <code>.was-validated</code> parent, no submit listener, no
    <code>classList</code>.
  </p>

  <DocsMeta id="validation" at="start" />

  <h2 class="h4">How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>:user-invalid</code> / <code>:user-valid</code> replace
      <code>.was-validated :invalid</code> / <code>:valid</code>. Empty
      required fields stay unstyled until the user interacts or submits.
      A non-required checkbox is always valid, so success green on
      <code>.form-check-input</code> only applies when the control is
      <code>required</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keep <code>.is-invalid</code> / <code>.is-valid</code> for
      server-side markup. They do not wait for interaction.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not add <code>novalidate</code>. Without script, that attribute
      lets an invalid form submit. Leave it off and the browser still
      blocks submit and may show its own bubble on the first invalid
      field.
    </li>
    <li>
      Custom <code>.invalid-feedback</code> / <code>.valid-feedback</code>
      (and the tooltip variants) must come <em>after</em> the control.
      CSS cannot style a label that sits before the input.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>.has-validation</code> on an input group is only for border
      radius when feedback is a sibling. It does not turn validation on.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>setCustomValidity</code> still needs JavaScript. Native
      constraints (<code>required</code>, <code>type</code>,
      <code>pattern</code>, <code>min</code> / <code>max</code>,
      <code>minlength</code> / <code>maxlength</code>) do not.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Example
    title="Custom styles"
    lead="Change a field and leave it, or press Submit. Borders, icons, and .valid-feedback / .invalid-feedback appear after that interaction — the same look as Bootstrap’s was-validated demo, without the class or the script."
    code={customCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Server-side"
    lead="When the server already knows the result, mark controls with .is-valid / .is-invalid. Associate each error with aria-describedby."
    code={serverCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Tooltips"
    lead="Swap .{valid|invalid}-feedback for .{valid|invalid}-tooltip. The parent needs position: relative (column classes already have it)."
    code={tooltipCode}
  />

  <DocsMeta id="validation" at="end" />
</BaseLayout>
